    {
      "title": "Check yourself",
      "role": "quiz",
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Check yourself",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "qa",
              "q": "In C++, what does `(-7 % 5)` give, and how do you get 3?",
              "a_bullets": [
                "It gives `-2`: the sign follows the left operand.",
                "Use `((a % m) + m) % m`, which gives `((-2) + 5) % 5 = 3`."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "qa",
              "q": "`int a = b = 100000; int l = a * b / gcd(a, b);` The true lcm is 100000. What goes wrong?",
              "a_bullets": [
                "`a * b` is 1e10, which overflows `int` before the division.",
                "Divide first: `a / gcd(a, b) * b`."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "qa",
              "q": "Someone computes the inverse of 5 mod 12 as `5^10 mod 12`. Why is that wrong?",
              "a_bullets": [
                "Fermat needs a prime modulus, and 12 is composite.",
                "`5^10 mod 12 = 1`, but `5 * 1 = 5`, not 1 mod 12. The real inverse is 5, found with extended Euclid."
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
